<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 4: Misuse and Misinterpretation  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--
  NOTES (see also notes/ch4-research.md):
  - Case study: Kenya's SHA means test (Lighthouse Reports, Africa Uncensored
    and the Guardian, May 2026; Africa Check, 25 November 2026). SHA disputes
    the findings. Lighthouse's figures come from its own rebuild of the model,
    and it says the rebuild may not match SHA's real system.
  - Podcasts: episode 21 (Dutch childcare scandal, COMPAS) and episode 65
    (Hallucitations). Dutch scandal facts are only from the podcast.
  - COMPAS facts are from ProPublica (2016), read in full.
  - Short Kenyan example: Judiciary draft AI directions (Nairobi Law Monthly,
    May 2026). Not used: the 2025 Lenaola and Magare rulings (only seen in
    search snippets), and the April 2026 Chigiti ruling (two reports disagree).
  - Practise (STACK) section removed on purpose. Questions go in the exercises.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-misuse-and-misinterpretation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Misuse and Misinterpretation</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-misuse-and-misinterpretation">
        <p>
            Peris Nduta used to do other people's washing in Dandora, a
            neighbourhood in Nairobi.
            One day she fell and broke her leg.
            At the public hospital she was told she needed to pay KES 7,800.
            Her friends raised less than half of it.
            She was sent home, and was brought back a week later for the operation
            (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            KES 7,800 is a year of health insurance, at KES 650 a month.
            It came from a formula.
            The formula doesn't ask what she earns.
            It looks at her home and her household, and estimates what
            she can afford.
        </p>

        <p>
            This chapter is about what happens when an AI tool, or any
            algorithm that makes predictions, is used without care, or when
            its answer is read as something it isn't.
            The tool can work as designed, and still do harm.
            A score is an estimate, and the trouble starts when people treat it
            as a fact.
        </p>

        <!-- Draft objectives: revise once the rest of the chapter is written. -->
        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Describe how the output of an algorithm can be misused
                        or misread in a real system.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Pick out what led to harm in cases like Kenya's
                        health insurance means test, COMPAS and the Dutch
                        childcare benefits scandal.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain the part played by human judgment,
                        accountability and the data behind a system.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Suggest ways to reduce the risk of misuse in your own
                        sector, community or context.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 1. Case study                                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: A Score for Every Household</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-problem">
            <title>How do you price insurance for people with no payslip?</title>

            <p>
                In October 2024, Kenya started a new public health insurance
                scheme.
                It replaced the old National Hospital Insurance Fund (NHIF)
                with the Social Health Authority, or SHA
                (<xref ref="biblio-capital-2026" text="custom">Wakaya, 2026</xref>).
                People with a salary pay 2.75% of it, taken by their employer
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenyans-2026" text="custom">Amunga, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Everyone else is meant to pay 2.75% of their household income,
                with a minimum of KES 300 a month
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenyans-2024" text="custom">Mito, 2024</xref>).
                That's most people.
                In 2025, about 18.1 million of the 21.6 million people in
                recorded employment, or 83.8%, worked in the informal sector,
                according to the Kenya National Bureau of Statistics
                (<xref ref="biblio-africacheck-2026" text="custom">Africa Check, 2026</xref>).
                A market trader or a casual labourer doesn't have a payslip.
                Their income goes up and down.
                So how does SHA know what to charge them?
            </p>

            <assemblage xml:id="box-misuse-and-misinterpretation-sha">
                <title>What is the money for?</title>
                <p>
                    Think of it as a shared pot.
                    Nobody knows who'll fall ill this year, so everyone pays a
                    little, and the pot pays the hospital bills of whoever
                    does.
                    The aim is that nobody is pushed into poverty, or turned
                    away, because they can't afford treatment.
                </p>
                <p>
                    SHA runs three funds from it
                    (<xref ref="biblio-africacheck-2026" text="custom">Africa Check, 2026</xref>).
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>SHIF</em> is the fund people pay into.
                            It covers most hospital care, including staying
                            in hospital.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>The Primary Healthcare Fund</em> pays for
                            basic care at dispensaries and health centres.
                            Everyone living in Kenya can use it, whether or
                            not they've paid.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>The Emergency, Chronic and Critical Illness
                            Fund</em> pays for emergency treatment.
                            Stabilising you and getting you to hospital is
                            covered whether or not you've paid, but only for
                            up to 24 hours.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    Does everyone pay?
                    Everyone has to register.
                    Not everyone pays.
                    If you have a salary, 2.75% comes out of it automatically.
                    If you don't, the means test works out what your household
                    can afford.
                    A household judged unable to pay can have its
                    contribution paid by the government or by a sponsor.
                    One person's contribution can also cover their family, such
                    as a spouse and children under 21
                    (<xref ref="biblio-africacheck-2026" text="custom">Africa Check, 2026</xref>).
                </p>
                <p>
                    It's called a contribution, not a tax.
                    But it's compulsory, so in practice it feels a lot like
                    one.
                    The difference is that your cover depends on keeping it
                    up to date.
                </p>
            </assemblage>

            <p>
                Its answer is a <term>proxy means test</term>.
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-misuse-and-misinterpretation-pmt">
                <title>Proxy means test</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        A proxy means test estimates how much a household can
                        afford without asking about its income.
                        Instead it asks about things that are easy to see,
                        like the roof, the toilet and the water supply, and
                        uses a formula to turn the answers into an estimate of
                        how well off the household is.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <p>
                The idea isn't silly.
                It was worked out at the World Bank in the mid-1990s, and
                versions of it are used in countries including Egypt,
                Indonesia and Rwanda.
                People who support it say it's an imperfect but practical way
                of getting help to the people who need it most.
                Critics say it's only moderately good at telling who's poor.
                One report for the Australian aid agency found that in four
                countries, between 44% and 71% of poor households were
                predicted not to be poor
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-how">
            <title>How the score works</title>

            <p>
                In October 2024, a graphic designer in western Kenya was
                registering online.
                The form asked him whether the walls of his house were
                plywood or mud, where his drinking water came from, whether
                his toilet flushed and whether he owned a bicycle.
                He couldn't see why.
                His answers went into a formula that worked out what he would
                pay
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                SHA hasn't published how its test works.
                In August 2025 the news team at Africa Uncensored sent SHA a
                request for the details.
                SHA ignored it, so the team complained to the Ombudsman, who
                ordered a reply.
                In November 2025 SHA sent back the formula and a list of the
                questions it uses, but not the weights it gives each answer
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The model uses 43 pieces of information about a household.
                They cover the household (its size, the mix of men, women,
                children and older people, and the county it lives in), the
                head of the household (their gender, marital status,
                education and work), and the home.
                For the home, the form asks about the roof, walls and floor,
                the toilet and water supply, what you cook and light your
                home with, the number of rooms, and whether you have
                electricity or own the place
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
                Urban and rural households are scored with different weights.
            </p>

            <p>
                The formula adds it all up into an estimate of how much the
                household spends per person, and the premium is 2.75% of that.
                Lighthouse says that spending is easier to measure than
                income, because people tend to report what they spend more
                accurately than what they earn.
                The weights were set using the 2021 Kenya Continuous Household
                Survey, which has about 17,000 households in it
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Is that AI?
                Lighthouse calls it machine learning, but a simple kind.
                It's a linear regression, which finds the best straight-line
                link between the answers and the outcome.
                That's much closer to a line drawn through survey data than to
                ChatGPT.
                The reporters and many people in Kenya call it AI.
                For this chapter that doesn't matter much, because a system that
                turns data into a score about a person can be misread in the
                same ways whatever we call it.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-findings">
            <title>What the investigation found</title>

            <p>
                In May 2026, Lighthouse Reports, Africa Uncensored and the
                Guardian published a joint investigation
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
                Because SHA hadn't given them the weights, Lighthouse rebuilt
                the model from the formula and the 2021 survey.
                They used 80% of the households to build it and kept the other
                20% back to test it.
            </p>

            <p>
                The first result is that the model is moderately good.
                It explains about two thirds of the differences in spending
                between urban households, and less than half in rural areas.
                Lighthouse says that's about what proxy means tests achieve
                in other countries.
                So the model does pick up something real.
            </p>

            <p>
                The second result is where it goes wrong.
                Lighthouse counted a prediction as correct if it was within
                about a fifth of the true figure.
                Among the poorest tenth of households, only 16% were predicted
                correctly, and 80% were predicted to be spending more than
                they really do.
                That means a bigger premium.
                Of the households in the poorest 40%, 39% were predicted to
                be above that line.
                In the rural test set, 400 of the 929 households below the
                poverty line, about 43%, were predicted to be above it.
                At the other end, the richest households tended to be
                predicted as spending less than they do.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-misuse-and-misinterpretation-deciles">
                <caption>How the rebuilt model's predictions compare with actual spending, by tenth of households. Data: Lighthouse Reports (2026), published results. Chart redrawn.</caption>
                <image source="images/ch4_accuracy_by_decile.svg" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        Stacked bar chart for ten groups of households from poorest to richest. In the poorest tenth, 81 percent are predicted to spend more than they do and 16 percent are accurate. The over-predicted share falls steadily to 8 percent in the richest tenth, where 60 percent are predicted to spend less than they do.
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-misuse-and-misinterpretation-exclusion">
                <caption>Poor households the rebuilt model placed above the poverty line. Data: Lighthouse Reports (2026), published results. Chart redrawn.</caption>
                <image source="images/ch4_exclusion_errors.svg" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        Bar chart. Of households below the poverty line in the test set, 30 percent of urban households, 43 percent of rural households and 39 percent overall were predicted to be above it.
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Lighthouse says this pattern is built into the method.
                Regression predicts towards the centre.
                The predictions bunch up in the middle, so the poorest end up
                looking better off and the richest worse off.
                It points to an academic study that found the same weakness in
                proxy means tests
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-misuse-and-misinterpretation-regression">
                <caption>A made-up illustration of why a model like this pulls towards the centre. These are not SHA or Lighthouse figures.</caption>
                <image source="images/ch4_regression_illustration.svg" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        Scatter plot of 240 made-up households. Actual spending is along the bottom and predicted spending up the side. The predictions are bunched more closely than the actual figures, and every household in the poorest tenth, shown in orange, sits above the dashed line, so is predicted to spend more than it does.
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Lighthouse is open about the limits of its work.
                Its rebuild may not match SHA's real system, because SHA may
                have cleaned or adjusted the data in ways it didn't disclose.
                The survey answers were collected by interviewers, while the
                real form is filled in online by households themselves, and
                that could change the results.
                SHA says it tries to pull some answers from government
                databases, to avoid people entering them by hand.
                SHA also didn't respond to the detailed questions that
                Lighthouse sent before publication.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-accounts">
            <title>Households behind the numbers</title>

            <p>
                The investigation spoke to people who had been given a
                premium.
                These are their accounts, as the reporters give them.
            </p>

            <assemblage>
                <title>Peris Nduta, Dandora, Nairobi</title>
                <p>
                    Until last year, Peris made a living doing other people's
                    washing.
                    After she broke her leg, she was told she needed to pay KES
                    7,800.
                    Friends raised less than half.
                    She was sent home, and brought back a week later for the
                    operation.
                    She could no longer work.
                    The reporters say she hasn't been able to afford more SHA
                    payments, or the care she needs afterwards.
                    She says it's because of her other responsibilities
                    (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).
                </p>
            </assemblage>

            <assemblage>
                <title>Florence Atieno, Huruma, Nairobi</title>
                <p>
                    Florence found the premium hard to pay while she was
                    pregnant.
                    She tried to appeal through an automated line, and the appeal
                    was turned down without a reason
                    (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
                </p>
            </assemblage>

            <assemblage>
                <title>Grace, a community health promoter in Nairobi</title>
                <p>
                    Grace works in Embakasi, in Nairobi, and her name has been
                    changed.
                    She says most of the people she helps have been assigned KES
                    650 a month.
                    For people below the poverty line, she says, that can be a
                    tenth of their income or more, and for people the statistics
                    bureau counts as food poor it is nearer a fifth.
                    She says that people are dying at home, and that many haven't
                    been able to go to hospital.
                    The reporters add that she has seen critically ill people who
                    couldn't get treatment because they couldn't pay.
                    Those are her accounts, and the reporting doesn't give a
                    hospital record for them
                    (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).
                </p>
            </assemblage>

            <p>
                Lighthouse also looked at individual households in the survey
                it used.
                These aren't SHA bills.
                They show what the rebuilt model would do.
            </p>

            <assemblage>
                <title>An elderly farming couple in Murang'a</title>
                <p>
                    They live in a modest home made of brick and iron sheets,
                    and drink rainwater.
                    They told the survey team that they sometimes skip meals.
                    The model puts their income higher, partly because they
                    have a covered toilet and a radio, and the head of the
                    household did a year at a vocational school.
                    They live far below the poverty line, but the model would
                    charge them more than two and a half times what it should
                    (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
                </p>
            </assemblage>

            <assemblage>
                <title>A young woman in Meru</title>
                <p>
                    She lives alone in a rented home and works as a farmhand,
                    walking about four kilometres to get to her job.
                    She said she eats less than she would like.
                    The model puts her income at nearly twice what it is,
                    partly because her home has wooden walls and electricity,
                    she finished high school, and she lives in Meru
                    (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
                </p>
            </assemblage>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-warnings">
            <title>Was anyone warned?</title>

            <p>
                Before the launch, SHA had the model checked by IDinsight,
                an international data consultancy.
                Its report came just before the launch.
                It said the model was unfair to low-income households, and
                that the survey behind it was out of date.
                Unless the model was retrained on new data, it said, the model
                was highly likely to get contributions wrong, especially for
                low-income households
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
                According to the reporters, it also said the survey had too
                many middle-income households and not enough very poor ones,
                and recommended that SHA manage the risk carefully at first,
                and that people be able to contest the prediction made about
                them
                (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                It wasn't a simple fix.
                IDinsight suggested a different design.
                Lighthouse tested it on the same survey, and found that it
                placed households in the right income band less often.
                The original model got 54% right, and the new design got 47%.
                Under both, nearly half of low-income households were
                predicted to have more income than they do
                (<xref ref="biblio-lighthouse-2026" text="custom">Lighthouse Reports, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The reporters also spoke to David Khaoya, a health economist
                who helped develop the first version of the test.
                According to the reporters, he said that the government decided
                to prioritise assessing the wealthy correctly, even if that meant
                overcharging the poor, and that poor households classed wrongly
                would be able to appeal
                (<xref ref="biblio-au-2026" text="custom">Africa Uncensored, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                This is a decision about who bears the errors.
                Every model makes mistakes.
                Someone chooses which mistakes are easier to live with, and
                in this telling the people who live with them are the poorest.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-response">
            <title>SHA's response, and the court</title>

            <p>
                SHA replied a few days after the documentary came out.
                It said that the model is fair and protects low-income
                households, that replacing the NHIF was needed, and that it
                will keep improving the tool as more household data comes in.
                It also gave figures.
                For households in the informal sector, it says, 92% are
                assessed at KES 850 a month or less, and 45% at between KES 300
                and KES 500
                (<xref ref="biblio-citizen-2026" text="custom">Nyambura, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Africa Uncensored says nothing in the reply answers its
                questions about how fair the model is to low-income
                households, or about the design choices behind it
                (<xref ref="biblio-ev-2026" text="custom">Eastleigh Voice, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <table xml:id="tbl-misuse-and-misinterpretation-disputed">
                <title>Two accounts of the same system</title>
                <tabular>
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell>The investigation</cell>
                        <cell>SHA</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>How well the formula sorts households</cell>
                        <cell>In the rebuilt model, 16% of the poorest tenth of
                        households were predicted correctly, and 80% were
                        overcharged</cell>
                        <cell>The model is fair and protects low-income
                        households</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>What households are charged</cell>
                        <cell>For people below the poverty line, KES 650 a
                        month can be a tenth of income or more (Grace's
                        account)</cell>
                        <cell>92% of informal households are assessed at KES 850
                        or less a month</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>The way forward</cell>
                        <cell>Answer questions about fairness and design
                        choices</cell>
                        <cell>Keep refining the tool as more data comes in</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                Look at the second row.
                Both of those things can be true at the same time.
                KES 650 is below KES 850, so it fits SHA's figures.
                SHA's numbers tell us how big the premiums are.
                They don't tell us whether those amounts are right for the
                people who pay them, or what they cost a family that earns very
                little.
            </p>

            <p>
                In March 2026, the High Court looked at the way SHIF was
                rolled out.
                It found the launch had been premature and unreasonable, and
                that it had denied patients critical care, including dialysis
                and cancer treatment.
                It decided not to cancel the scheme, because millions of
                people were already signed up.
                The ruling was about the rollout as a whole, and it didn't
                judge the means-test model itself.
                But it ordered fair means-testing for informal workers, a clear
                and accessible complaints mechanism, and regular progress
                reports to the court
                (<xref ref="biblio-capital-2026" text="custom">Wakaya, 2026</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-registered">
            <title>Registered is not the same as covered</title>

            <p>
                There's a second way that numbers get misread in this story.
                On 18 November 2026, in his state of the nation address, the
                President said that 33.7 million Kenyans are now registered
                with SHA.
                He told the story of a mother of three, a small-scale farmer,
                who had a kidney transplant.
                She paid KES 840 a month, and SHA paid her bill of KES 966,000,
                not counting dialysis
                (<xref ref="biblio-africacheck-2026" text="custom">Africa Check, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Africa Check looked at the figure.
                Being registered isn't the same as paying, and paying isn't the
                same as being covered.
                SHA's own report for the period to 31 March 2026 shows 30.13
                million registered beneficiaries, and about 4.78 million active
                contributors.
                That doesn't mean only one in seven people has cover, because
                one contributor can cover a whole family.
                But SHA hasn't published how many registered people have active
                cover, and Africa Check says it asked and hadn't had an
                answer when it published
                (<xref ref="biblio-africacheck-2026" text="custom">Africa Check, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-misuse-and-misinterpretation-registered">
                <caption>Registered is not the same as paying. Data: Africa Check (2026), citing the President's address and SHA's impact report. Chart redrawn.</caption>
                <image source="images/ch4_registered_vs_paying.svg" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        Bar chart in millions of people. 33.7 million are registered, as the President said. SHA's report to 31 March 2026 shows 30.13 million registered beneficiaries and 4.78 million active contributors.
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                A government survey, the 2025 to 2026 Kenya Mini Demographic and
                Health Survey, gives another view.
                Of the people registered as contributing members, only 43.9%
                said their premium had been paid that month.
                It also found a gap by wealth.
                In the poorest fifth of households, 24% of people reported
                having SHA cover, compared with 49.2% in the richest fifth.
                Among contributing members, 17.9% in the poorest fifth were up
                to date, compared with 65.9% in the richest
                (<xref ref="biblio-africacheck-2026" text="custom">Africa Check, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-misuse-and-misinterpretation-wealth-gap">
                <caption>Cover and payment by household wealth. Data: Africa Check (2026), citing the 2025 to 2026 Kenya Mini Demographic and Health Survey. Chart redrawn.</caption>
                <image source="images/ch4_wealth_gap.svg" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        Grouped bar chart. In the poorest fifth of households, 24 percent report having SHA cover and 17.9 percent of contributing members are paid up. In the richest fifth, 49.2 percent report cover and 65.9 percent are paid up.
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The rules matter too.
                Unpaid contributions attract a 2% monthly penalty, and arrears
                must be cleared before someone can use SHIF-funded services
                again.
                Emergency stabilisation is available whether or not you've
                paid, but for up to 24 hours
                (<xref ref="biblio-africacheck-2026" text="custom">Africa Check, 2026</xref>).
                So someone who is registered and behind on payments isn't left
                with no care at all, because primary care is still open to
                everyone.
                But most hospital care is paid for through SHIF, and that
                depends on being up to date.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-lessons">
            <title>What the case shows</title>

            <p>
                Nobody in this story needed to be a villain.
                SHA had a real problem to solve, and a proxy means test is a
                reasonable idea.
                The harm came from how the score was used.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>An estimate was treated as a bill.</em>
                        The formula says what a household is likely to be able to
                        pay, but it was used to decide what a household
                        owes.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>The data didn't match the people.</em>
                        The survey behind the model had few very poor
                        households in it, so the model knew least about the
                        people it could hurt most.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>The errors weren't spread evenly.</em>
                        They fell on the poorest households, and that was a
                        choice, even if it wasn't made in public.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>A warning arrived and wasn't enough.</em>
                        Someone checked the model before it went live.
                        What they found didn't change the launch.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>A headline number can hide who's missing.</em>
                        33.7 million people were registered, but far fewer
                        were paying, and the poorest were the least likely to
                        be up to date.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>A way to challenge it has to work.</em>
                        An appeal that gets an automatic refusal isn't an
                        appeal in any real sense.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    A tool can be accurate on average and still harm the
                    people it knows least about.
                    It's not enough to ask whether the model works.
                    We also need to ask who it works for, and who pays when it
                    doesn't.
                </p>
            </insight>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 2. Podcast: risk is not reality                                -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-risk">
        <title>Risk Is Not Reality</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-misuse-and-misinterpretation-risk">
            <p>
                SHA is not the first time this has happened.
                The ideas in this section come from a conversation between me
                and David Stern, from the IDEMS Podcast
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-021" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 21, n.d.</xref>).
                It's about twenty-four minutes long.
                It's also available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/021-where-things-have-gone-wrong-with-ai/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-misuse-and-misinterpretation-podcast-021">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 21: Where Things Have Gone Wrong with AI</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-misuse-and-misinterpretation-podcast-021"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/3360/021-where-things-have-gone-wrong-with-ai.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-dutch">
            <title>The Dutch childcare scandal</title>

            <p>
                The episode starts with the Dutch childcare benefits
                scandal.
                In the Netherlands, an algorithm was used to decide whether
                people might be claiming childcare benefits fraudulently.
                As we say in the episode, the consequences were horrific.
                Children were taken away and put into care, families had
                serious money problems, and there were suicides and divorces.
                David is clear that he doesn't believe anybody meant any harm.
            </p>

            <p>
                The first point is about the word <q>determine</q>.
                In the episode I say that the algorithm was used to determine
                whether someone was committing fraud, and then correct myself,
                because it should only ever have said who was at risk of it.
                David agrees, and goes further.
                An algorithm can raise a red flag.
                It can't know what's actually going on in a household.
                In his view, treating a risk as if it were reality is a
                <q>cardinal sin</q>.
            </p>

            <p>
                He gave an example.
                A system that predicts who's likely to commit a crime might
                be a reason to keep an eye on someone.
                But it could never be a reason to put them in jail, because
                they haven't done anything, and they could still choose not to.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-response-design">
            <title>What do you do with a red flag?</title>

            <p>
                David's second point is that a risk score doesn't tell you
                what to do next.
                A family flagged as being at risk of fraud could be sent an
                investigator.
                They could also be sent a care worker, because the risk might
                come from the difficult situation they're in.
            </p>

            <p>
                That matters a lot when the data has biases in it.
                If you send an investigator, and the system flags some
                minority groups more often, those groups get over-checked,
                and that's not acceptable.
                If you send a care worker, the same bias might mean those
                groups get more support than they need.
                David says that's a much smaller cost, and could even help to
                correct for past under-support.
                He's careful to say that he doesn't have the answers, and that
                each case needs deep thought.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    The same score can lead to very different outcomes,
                    depending on what the people using it do next.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                You can see the same thing in SHA.
                A household was given a score, and the next step was a bill.
                If the next step had been a conversation, the same score
                could have done far less harm.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-compas">
            <title>COMPAS</title>

            <p>
                We also talk about COMPAS, which comes up in the US courts.
                COMPAS is a risk score, made by a company called Northpointe,
                that estimates how likely a person is to commit a crime in
                future.
                ProPublica measured that as being arrested again within two
                years.
                It's based on a questionnaire of 137 items.
                Race isn't one of the questions
                (<xref ref="biblio-propublica-method-2016" text="custom">Angwin et al., 2016</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                In 2016, the journalists at ProPublica looked at what happened to
                more than 7,000 people who'd been scored in Broward County,
                Florida.
                They compared the scores to who was arrested again over the
                next two years.
                They gave two examples.
                Brisha Borden, who was 18 and Black, and Vernon Prater, who was
                41 and white, were each arrested for a small theft.
                Hers was a bike and a scooter worth about $80 (about KES 10,400
                at the 2026 rate).
                Borden was rated high risk, and Prater low risk.
                Prater had already been convicted of armed robbery before.
                Borden had only minor offences as a juvenile.
                Two years later, Borden hadn't been charged with anything new,
                and Prater was in prison for breaking into a warehouse
                (<xref ref="biblio-propublica-2016" text="custom">Angwin et al., 2016</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                ProPublica's main finding was that the score was wrong in
                different ways for different people.
                Among Black defendants who didn't go on to be arrested again,
                45% had been labelled higher risk.
                Among white defendants it was 23%.
                White defendants who did go on to reoffend were more likely to
                have been labelled low risk
                (<xref ref="biblio-propublica-method-2016" text="custom">Angwin et al., 2016</xref>).
                Of the people the score flagged as likely to reoffend, 61% were
                arrested again, and for violent crime it was only 20%
                (<xref ref="biblio-propublica-2016" text="custom">Angwin et al., 2016</xref>).
                Northpointe said it didn't agree that ProPublica's results were
                correct.
                It didn't share its calculations, which it said are
                proprietary.
            </p>

            <p>
                The article is also a story about how scores get used.
                In Wisconsin, a judge threw out a plea deal and gave a man
                called Paul Zilly a longer sentence, after seeing a high risk
                score.
                The score's own creator, Tim Brennan, then told the court
                that it shouldn't be the only basis for a decision, and
                the sentence was cut
                (<xref ref="biblio-propublica-2016" text="custom">Angwin et al., 2016</xref>).
                That's the same point David makes about the Dutch case.
                The score was meant to be one piece of information.
            </p>

            <p>
                David points out that COMPAS is a different case.
                The people being scored have already been arrested, so the
                question is about how lenient to be.
                He says that whether it should be used for that at all is a
                separate question, and one for another episode.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-layers">
            <title>Keep the people in</title>

            <p>
                What should you do instead?
                In the episode, David goes back to the post office example from
                our earlier episode on demystifying data science.
                If getting the post to the right place were a matter of life
                or death, you wouldn't replace the human sorters with a
                machine that's right 90% of the time.
                You'd keep the people, and put the machine on top.
                It costs more, but it's much safer.
                It might even make the sorters' job easier, because most of
                the work has already been done.
            </p>

            <p>
                He mentions a study where people seemed to check another
                person's work more carefully than they checked an AI's.
                He hasn't looked into it properly, and nor have I, so treat it
                as something to look up.
                The point he makes from it is that the way you fit AI into a
                system changes how people behave in that system.
            </p>

            <p>
                The part of the conversation that stays with me is at the end.
                David says there are two sides to <q>do no harm</q>.
                One is not harming people on purpose, which is in your
                control.
                The other is that even when you're trying to do things right,
                the consequences of what you build can be out of your hands.
                He says we need AI literacy at every level, from the people who
                build these systems to their managers and the people who use
                them.
                He's honest that he doesn't think the tools exist yet to
                stop this happening again.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-risk-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A score is an estimate of risk, and it isn't a fact about
                        a person.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What matters just as much is what people do next with
                        the score.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Bias in the data can lead to harm that is spread
                        unevenly.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Keeping people in the process, as a second layer, is
                        safer than handing the decision over.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 3. Podcast: made-up citations                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-citations">
        <title>Made-Up Citations</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-misuse-and-misinterpretation-citations">
            <p>
                Misuse isn't only about scores.
                It's also about trusting a tool to do something it wasn't built
                to do.
                This section comes from another conversation between me and
                David
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-065" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 65, n.d.</xref>).
                It's about twenty-six minutes long.
                It's also available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/065-hallucitations/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-misuse-and-misinterpretation-podcast-065">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 65: Hallucitations</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-misuse-and-misinterpretation-podcast-065"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/3971/065-hallucitations.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-hallucitations">
            <title>Hallucitations</title>

            <p>
                A hallucitation is what you get when an AI chat tool, such as
                ChatGPT, is asked for citations and makes them up.
                It gives you an author's name and the title of a paper, and it
                all looks right.
                Then you search for the paper, and it doesn't exist.
                I've been surprised by how often that happens.
            </p>

            <p>
                It has also hurt real people.
                In the episode I mention a law professor who found that an AI
                tool had named him in a story about professors who'd abused
                their students.
                He'd never been to that school or on that trip.
                He found it funny at first, and then he realised it was
                defamation.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-why-citations">
            <title>Why does it do this?</title>

            <p>
                David's explanation is that a tool like ChatGPT is trying to
                write something that looks like what you want to read.
                It doesn't understand what it's saying.
                When you ask for a citation, it puts one together from pieces
                of text from all over the internet.
                It isn't searching a list of real cases.
                Sometimes the pieces make a real citation, and sometimes they
                don't.
            </p>

            <p>
                He thinks the problem can be solved, but not by making the
                same model bigger.
                You'd need a design with two layers, where a separate part of
                the system looks up real sources and doesn't make anything
                up.
                He gives another example of two layers working well, where
                people building an AI maths tutor got better results by
                first getting the AI to work out the answer, and then using
                that answer to explain it.
                David describes it as thinking before you speak.
            </p>

            <p>
                So why hasn't it been fixed?
                David thinks it's about money.
                Building a more reliable system would be expensive, and the
                companies don't have a strong reason to do it, unless the
                cost of the lawsuits gets very large.
                He says clearly that he isn't arguing for that.
                He's just describing the incentives.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-powerless">
            <title>What about people who can't fight back?</title>

            <p>
                The people in the stories I mentioned were well known, or
                had the means to complain.
                David asks about everyone else.
                If a made-up statement about you appears, and you don't have
                a lawyer or a platform, how do you show it's false?
                And would anyone ever notice?
                He says he's more worried about this than he is about the
                robots that people talk about at AI safety summits.
            </p>

            <p>
                His answer for now is that people should stay in the role of
                the critic.
                You can use the tool, but you should check what comes out, and
                you're the one who's responsible for it.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-kenya-courts">
            <title>Closer to home: Kenya's courts</title>

            <p>
                Kenya's courts are dealing with the same problem.
                In 2026, Nairobi Law Monthly reported that several judges had
                struck out pleadings containing AI-generated text and cases
                that don't exist.
                It also reported that the Judiciary is drafting an AI policy
                and practice directions.
                The draft was sent to advocates for public participation, and
                the Law Society of Kenya has invited feedback from its members.
                It says that advocates who use AI to prepare court documents
                must say so when they file.
                They must check every authority they cite, and they mustn't
                file anything written wholly by an AI tool without proper
                human review.
                Judicial officers must treat what an AI gives them as
                information only
                (<xref ref="biblio-nlm-2026" text="custom">Muthoni, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Read that again and you can hear David's advice from the
                episode.
                The tool can help, and the person is still responsible.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-citations-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A tool that writes something that looks right isn't
                        checking that it is right.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Made-up citations have caused real harm, including
                        defamation, and in Kenya the courts are now writing rules
                        about them.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Better design is possible, but it costs money, so the
                        incentives matter.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        For now, the person using the tool is the one who has to
                        check it.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 4. Illustrative example                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-misuse-and-misinterpretation-worked-example">
        <title>Try It: Checking an AI's Citations</title>

        <p>
            Let's try this for ourselves.
            You'll need an AI assistant, and a way to search for articles,
            like Google Scholar.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Pick a topic you know a bit about.
                    Ask the assistant for five sources on it, with authors,
                    titles and years.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Look up each one.
                    Does the paper exist?
                    Is it by those authors, in that year?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If it exists, find out whether it really says what the
                    assistant said it says.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Write down how many were real, how many were real but
                    misdescribed, and how many you couldn't find.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Please don't put anyone's personal details into the assistant.
            If you can't find a source, don't assume that's because you
            searched badly.
            Try more than one search tool before you decide it doesn't exist.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 5. Exercises                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-misuse-and-misinterpretation">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-misuse-and-misinterpretation-1">
            <title>Pick a sector</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    So far we've looked at systems where AI caused harm.
                    But AI is also being used in Kenya in ways that could help a
                    lot.
                    Here are some sectors.
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Health, where apps and bots can help people
                                understand their symptoms (for example
                                Amenya), or offer mental health support (for
                                example Chat Care from the Kenya Red Cross).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Education, such as Angaza Elimu, a classroom tool
                                for teachers and students.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Agriculture, where platforms give farmers advice
                                on crops, weather, pests and prices (for example
                                FarmerAI).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Finance, where AI helps to spot fraud (for
                                example Safaricom's use of machine learning).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Conservation, where cameras and drones help to
                                track endangered animals.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                    Choose one, and think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            How could AI help in this sector, in Kenya?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What harm could happen if it isn't used properly?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Are there measures in place now to prevent that?
                            If not in Kenya, what's done elsewhere?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Where could things go wrong: in the design of the
                            algorithm, in the data, or in how people read and
                            use what it says?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you suggest to prevent the harm?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-misuse-and-misinterpretation-2">
            <title>An investigator or a care worker?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Think about a system that flags households as being at
                    risk, whether of fraud, of missing school fees or of not
                    being able to pay a health premium.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What would happen to a family if an investigator
                            arrived?
                            What if a care worker did?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who decides which of them is sent?
                            Should that person be the one who built the
                            system?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            If the system is wrong about some groups more often
                            than others, which of the two responses is safer?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-misuse-and-misinterpretation-3">
            <title>Who carries the errors?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    David Khaoya says that officials chose to get the
                    better-off households right, even if that meant the poor
                    were overcharged.
                    Every model makes mistakes.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            If a model had to be wrong about someone, who
                            should it be wrong about, and why?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who should decide, and how would the public know?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Would your answer change if the cost of being
                            wrong was different, say a small bill instead of
                            being turned away from treatment?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-misuse-and-misinterpretation-4">
            <title>Is KES 650 a lot?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    SHA says that 92% of informal households are assessed at
                    KES 850 a month or less.
                    Grace, the community health promoter, says that KES 650
                    can be a tenth of income or more for people below the
                    poverty line.
                    The premium is 2.75% of the income the formula predicts.
                    What monthly income does a KES 650 premium assume?
                    How can both statements be true?
                    What numbers would you ask for to decide whether the
                    premiums are fair?
                    Who would have them?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-misuse-and-misinterpretation-5">
            <title>What makes an appeal real?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Florence's request for a review was turned down by an
                    automated reply, with no reason.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What would an appeals process need, for you to
                            trust it?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Think about the person who might use it.
                            What might stop them?
                            Think about cost, time, and what they'd need to
                            know first.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who should read the appeals, a person or a
                            machine?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-misuse-and-misinterpretation-6">
            <title>A second layer</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    David says that if the post were a matter of life or
                    death, you'd keep the human sorters and put the machine on
                    top.
                    Pick a decision in your own sector that an AI tool could
                    help with.
                    Describe the two layers.
                    What would the people do, what would the tool do, and
                    what would they do when they disagree?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-misuse-and-misinterpretation-7">
            <title>What did you find?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In the Try It, how many of the assistant's sources were real,
                    and how many said what it claimed?
                    How did it sound when it was wrong?
                    Would you have noticed if you hadn't checked?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-misuse-and-misinterpretation-8">
            <title>A note to SHA</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Imagine you've been asked to advise SHA after the
                    investigation.
                    Write a short note, no more than a page, saying what you
                    would change first, and why.
                    Say which of these problems your changes would fix: the
                    data, the way the score was designed, or the way people
                    read and used it.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-misuse-and-misinterpretation-9">
            <title>Registered or covered?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The President said that 33.7 million Kenyans are registered
                    with SHA.
                    SHA's own report shows about 4.78 million active
                    contributors.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Why can't we divide one number by the other to find
                            out how many people have cover?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What number would you ask SHA for?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Find a headline number from your own work or
                            community that could be read in more than one way.
                            What does it count, and what does it leave out?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- Sources                                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <references xml:id="references-misuse-and-misinterpretation">
        <title>Sources</title>

        <biblio type="raw" xml:id="biblio-lighthouse-2026">
            Lighthouse Reports (2026). How we investigated Kenya's AI means
            testing system. 4 May 2026. Methodology for the investigation
            <q>Hiding behind AI</q>, published with Africa Uncensored and The
            Guardian.
            <url href="https://www.lighthousereports.com/methodology/how-we-investigated-kenyas-ai-means-testing-system/"/>.
        </biblio>

        <biblio type="raw" xml:id="biblio-africacheck-2026">
